export const G1={title:'The surgical technologist profession and the event',hook:'In every operation, one person stands across from the surgeon, anticipates the next instrument, guards the sterile field and keeps count of every sponge and needle. That person is often a certified surgical technologist. The profession began with military operating-room technicians in World War II and today is essential to more than 50 million surgeries a year in the United States. The profession of the CST is 10% of the test.',objectives:['Describe the Surgical Technologist event, its test plan and its six skills.','Trace the history, education and certification of surgical technologists.','Describe the roles of the surgical team, including first scrub and circulator.','Apply ethical and legal principles, including surgical conscience, consent and the Universal Protocol.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Surgical Technologist has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: **instrumentation, equipment and supplies 30%**, **surgical asepsis and sterility 20%**, **surgical anatomy and physiology 20%**, **profession of the CST 10%**, **physical environment and safety standards 10%** and **hemostasis, wound healing and wound closure 10%**. References are the AST’s *Surgical Technology for the Surgical Technologist: A Positive Care Approach* (the test focuses on chapters 1, 4, 7, 10, 11 and 16) and Nemitz’s *Surgical Instrumentation*.',
'**Round Two** may include: **I** identify instruments/equipment (15 minutes; 15 items, name and spelling plus purpose; may be given with the test), **II** surgical scrub (6), **III** open gloving (3), **IV** gowning and gloving self (5), **V** passing surgical instruments (5) and **VI** loading and passing sutures (4). Bring photo ID, **two pairs of sterile surgical gloves**, a surgical or bouffant cap, a **level 3 sterile surgical gown**, shoe covers, a **surgical tie mask**, eye protection and a **surgical scrub brush without soap**. You need **70%** on the Round Two skills (excluding the test and instrument ID); ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Instrumentation, equipment and supplies','30%'],['Surgical asepsis and sterility','20%'],['Surgical anatomy and physiology','20%'],['Profession of the CST','10%'],['Physical environment and safety standards','10%'],['Hemostasis, wound healing and wound closure','10%']]}]],
['History and credentials',[
'During **World War II**, military medics trained as **operating room technicians** to help surgeons when nurses were in short supply. After the war, hospitals adopted the role. The **Association of Surgical Technologists (AST)** began in **1969** (as the Association of Operating Room Technicians) and sponsors this HOSA event. Certification began in 1970; today the **National Board of Surgical Technology and Surgical Assisting (NBSTSA)** awards the **Certified Surgical Technologist (CST)** credential and the **Certified Surgical First Assistant (CSFA)** credential.',
'Surgical technology programs—certificate, diploma or associate degree—are accredited by **CAAHEP** through the **ARC/STSA**. Many states require certification or registration to practice. CSTs earn continuing education credits to stay certified. Job growth is steady because of the number of surgeries and an aging population.',
order('Path to becoming a CST',['High school science courses','Accredited surgical technology program','Clinical rotations in the operating room','Pass the NBSTSA CST exam','State requirements and employment','Continuing education; CSFA as an advanced option'])]],
['The surgical team',[
'The **sterile team** includes the **surgeon**, the **surgical first assistant** (CSFA, PA, resident or RN first assistant) and the **surgical technologist in the first scrub role**, who prepares the sterile field, sets up instruments, passes instruments and supplies, anticipates the surgeon’s needs, maintains sterility, performs counts with the circulator and handles specimens on the field. The **non-sterile team** includes the **circulator** (usually a registered nurse, who manages the room, documents, opens supplies, assists with positioning and counts), the **anesthesia provider** (anesthesiologist or certified registered nurse anesthetist) and others such as radiology technologists and perfusionists.',
'CSTs may also work in the **second scrub** or **assistant circulator** role, in central sterile processing, labor and delivery, ambulatory surgery centers, organ procurement and as educators or sales representatives. Teamwork and clear communication—using **closed-loop communication** (repeat back what was asked)—prevent errors.',
"The CST’s day includes **preoperative** duties (checking the surgeon’s preference card, gathering supplies, opening sterile packs with the circulator, scrubbing, gowning, gloving and setting up), **intraoperative** duties (counts, passing, holding retractors, managing specimens and medications on the field) and **postoperative** duties (dressings, breaking down the field, safe sharps disposal and preparing instruments for processing). Preference cards list each surgeon’s preferred instruments, sutures and gloves.",
sort('Sterile or non-sterile team member?',['Sterile team','Non-sterile team'],[['Surgeon',0],['CST in the first scrub role',0],['Circulator',1],['Anesthesiologist',1],['Surgical first assistant',0],['Radiology technologist',1]])]],
['Surgical conscience and ethics',[
'**Surgical conscience** is an inner commitment to strict **aseptic technique** and honesty—admitting and correcting any break in sterility immediately, even if no one else saw it, because the patient’s safety depends on it. CSTs follow the **AST Code of Ethics**: respect patient dignity and privacy (including the unconscious patient), maintain confidentiality, practice competently within their scope and report unsafe or unethical behavior.',
'Patients must give **informed consent** for surgery, obtained by the surgeon; the circulator verifies the signed consent. **Advance directives** and DNR orders are reviewed with the team. Legal principles include **negligence**, the **standard of care**, **respondeat superior** (employers are responsible for employees’ actions at work) and the **captain of the ship** doctrine (historically, the surgeon’s responsibility for the team). Documentation and counts protect patients and staff.',
match('Professional terms',[['Surgical conscience','Honest commitment to correct any break in sterility'],['Informed consent','Patient agrees after learning risks, benefits and alternatives'],['Respondeat superior','Employer responsible for employees’ actions at work'],['Closed-loop communication','Repeat back requests to confirm'],['Scope of practice','Legal limits of a professional’s role'],['Confidentiality','Protecting patient information']])]],
['Preventing wrong-site surgery and retained items',[
'The Joint Commission’s **Universal Protocol** has three parts: **pre-procedure verification** (correct patient, procedure, site, consent, imaging and equipment), **site marking** by the person performing the procedure, and a **time-out** immediately before the incision in which the whole team stops and agrees on the patient, procedure, site and other key facts. The WHO Surgical Safety Checklist adds sign-in and sign-out steps.',
'**Counts** of sponges, sharps and instruments prevent retained surgical items. Counts are done **before the procedure** (baseline), **before closing a cavity within a cavity**, **before closing the wound** (and at skin closure) and **whenever personnel change** (permanent relief). The CST and circulator count aloud together, and any discrepancy is reported immediately and searched for before closing; an X-ray may be taken.',
order('When to count',['Before the procedure begins (baseline)','Before closing a cavity within a cavity','Before closing the wound','At skin closure','When scrub or circulating personnel change permanently'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Surgical conscience',{a:{text:'While setting up, you notice your glove brushed the edge of a non-sterile table. No one else saw it. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Announce it and change your glove immediately','b','Correct. Surgical conscience means correcting every break.',true],['Ignore it because it was brief','b','Any contamination must be corrected.',false]]},b:{text:'During the final count, one lap sponge is missing. What happens next?',choices:[['Report the discrepancy, stop closure and search; an X-ray may be ordered','c','Correct. Never close with an unresolved count.',true],['Close anyway and recount later','c','Retained items harm patients.',false]]},c:{text:'The sponge is found in the kick bucket. The circulator documents the correct count, and closure proceeds.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One: instruments 30%, asepsis 20%, anatomy 20%, profession, environment and hemostasis 10% each; Round Two has six skills; bring sterile gloves, cap, level 3 gown, shoe covers, tie mask, eye protection and a scrub brush.','Surgical technology began with WWII OR technicians; the AST began in 1969; the NBSTSA awards the CST and CSFA; the first scrub CST sets up, passes, anticipates, counts and guards the sterile field while the circulator (RN) manages the room.','Surgical conscience means admitting every break in sterility; the Universal Protocol (verification, site marking, time-out) and counts at set times prevent wrong-site surgery and retained items.'],terms:[['CST','Certified Surgical Technologist.'],['Surgical conscience','A personal commitment to strict aseptic technique and honesty.'],['Time-out','A pause before incision when the team confirms patient, procedure and site.']],mcqs:[q('Which area is the largest part of the Surgical Technologist test?','Instrumentation, equipment and supplies','Profession of the CST','Hemostasis and wound closure','Physical environment','30%.'),q('Which organization awards the CST credential?','NBSTSA','AST','CAAHEP','AORN','National board.'),q('Who usually serves as the circulator?','A registered nurse','The surgeon','The CST in the first scrub role','The patient’s family','Non-sterile team.'),q('Surgical conscience means:','Admitting and correcting any break in sterility','Working quickly','Following the surgeon’s preferences only','Never speaking in the OR','Honesty about asepsis.'),q('The time-out happens:','Immediately before the incision','After the surgery','In the recovery room','The day before surgery','All team members participate.'),q('When is a count required?','Before closing the wound','Only at the start','Only if the surgeon asks','Never','Also before closing a cavity and at personnel changes.'),q('Surgical technologists began as military:','Operating room technicians in World War II','Pilots','Nurses only','Pharmacists','Wartime shortage.'),q('What should you bring for Skill II?','A surgical scrub brush without soap','A bar of soap','Hand sanitizer only','Nothing','Per the guidelines.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the roles of the CST in the first scrub role and the circulator, and explain how surgical conscience, the Universal Protocol and counts protect surgical patients.',rubric:[['First scrub role',['sterile field','set up','pass','anticipate','instruments','specimen','count']],['Circulator role',['circulator','registered nurse','non-sterile','document','open supplies','positioning','count']],['Surgical conscience',['surgical conscience','break','sterility','admit','correct','honest']],['Universal Protocol and counts',['verification','site marking','time-out','counts','before closure','personnel change','retained']]],model:'In the first scrub role, the CST works within the sterile field: setting up the back table and Mayo stand, gowning and gloving the surgeon and assistant, passing instruments, sutures and supplies, anticipating the surgeon’s next need, handling specimens on the field and maintaining sterility. The circulator, usually a registered nurse, is part of the non-sterile team. The circulator manages the room, assists with positioning, opens sterile supplies onto the field, documents the procedure and performs counts with the CST. Surgical conscience is the CST’s commitment to admit and correct any break in sterile technique immediately—even when no one else notices—so contaminated items are replaced and infections are prevented. The Universal Protocol prevents wrong-patient, wrong-procedure and wrong-site surgery through pre-procedure verification of the patient, procedure, consent and site, site marking by the surgeon and a time-out just before the incision in which the whole team confirms the details. Counts of sponges, sharps and instruments—done aloud by the CST and circulator before the procedure, before closing a cavity, before closing the wound, at skin closure and when personnel change—prevent retained surgical items, and any discrepancy is reported and resolved before the wound is closed.'},sources:[RULES,AST,NBSTSA,BLS_ST,TJC_UP]};

export const G2={title:'The operating room environment and safety standards',hook:'An operating room is designed to fight infection: filtered air pushes contaminants out, traffic is controlled and temperature and humidity are kept in narrow ranges. It is also full of hazards—electrosurgical units, lasers, oxygen, radiation, chemicals and sharp instruments. Surgical fires still happen in the United States every year. The physical environment and safety standards are 10% of the test.',objectives:['Describe the OR suite design, traffic zones and environmental controls.','Explain electrosurgery and the fire triangle and how to prevent surgical fires.','Apply radiation, laser, chemical, sharps and ergonomic safety.','Position patients safely and recognize positioning injuries.'],sections:[
['OR design and traffic',[
'The surgical suite has three zones. The **unrestricted zone** (entrance, locker rooms, front desk) allows street clothes. The **semirestricted zone** (hallways, sterile storage, processing areas) requires **scrub attire and head covering**. The **restricted zone** (operating rooms and sterile core) requires scrub attire, head covering and **masks** when sterile supplies are open or scrubbed personnel are present. Traffic is kept to a minimum, and doors stay closed to maintain air pressure.',
'ORs use **positive air pressure** (air flows out of the room) with at least **20 total air exchanges per hour** through high-efficiency filters, a **temperature of about 68–75 °F** (20–24 °C) and **relative humidity of about 20–60%**. Too much humidity encourages microbial growth; too little increases static electricity. Floors and surfaces are cleaned between cases and **terminally cleaned** at the end of the day.',
"Room cleaning between cases—**turnover**—includes removing trash and linens, disinfecting all horizontal surfaces and equipment that touched the patient and wet-vacuuming or mopping the floor around the OR table. **Standard precautions** apply to every patient, so the same thorough cleaning is done after every case, regardless of the diagnosis.",
sort('Which zone?',['Unrestricted','Semirestricted','Restricted'],[['Locker room in street clothes',0],['Hallway to the ORs with scrubs and hair covered',1],['Inside an OR with sterile supplies open',2],['Front desk',0],['Sterile storage area',1],['Sterile core',2]])]],
['Electrosurgery',[
'The **electrosurgical unit (ESU)** uses high-frequency current to **cut** tissue and **coagulate** bleeding vessels. In **monopolar** electrosurgery, current flows from the **active electrode** (the **electrosurgical pencil**) through the patient to a **patient return electrode** (dispersive or “grounding” pad) and back to the generator. The pad is placed on clean, dry skin over a **large, well-perfused muscle mass** close to the surgical site—avoiding **bony prominences**, scar tissue, hair, tattoos and **metal implants**—to prevent burns. In **bipolar** electrosurgery, current passes only between the two tips of a forceps, so no return pad is needed.',
'Safety steps: place the active electrode in an **insulated holster** when not in use, keep it clean of eschar (charred tissue), use the lowest effective settings, check cords for damage and never activate it near flammable vapors. **Smoke evacuation** removes surgical plume, which can contain harmful chemicals and particles.',
match('ESU terms',[['Active electrode','Electrosurgical pencil that delivers current'],['Patient return electrode','Dispersive pad that collects current'],['Monopolar','Current travels through the patient to a pad'],['Bipolar','Current flows between forceps tips only'],['Holster','Insulated holder for the pencil when not in use'],['Smoke evacuator','Removes surgical plume']])]],
['Surgical fire prevention',[
'Fire requires the **fire triangle**: an **ignition source** (ESU, laser, fiber-optic light cords, drills), **fuel** (drapes, gowns, sponges, **alcohol-based prep solutions**, hair, ointments) and an **oxidizer** (**oxygen** and **nitrous oxide**). Most surgical fires occur in oxygen-enriched environments around the head, neck and upper chest. The surgeon controls ignition sources, the anesthesia provider controls oxidizers and the nursing and scrub team control fuels.',
'Prevention: allow **alcohol-based preps to dry completely** (often at least 3 minutes) and prevent pooling; use the lowest oxygen concentration possible for head and neck procedures; keep light cords off drapes; moisten sponges near ignition sources; and keep saline or water on the field. If a fire occurs, **stop the flow of gases**, remove burning materials, extinguish the fire (saline or a CO₂ extinguisher) and care for the patient. The OR fire plan follows RACE and PASS.',
sort('Part of the fire triangle',['Ignition source','Fuel','Oxidizer'],[['Electrosurgical pencil',0],['Alcohol prep solution',1],['Oxygen',2],['Surgical drapes',1],['Laser',0],['Nitrous oxide',2]])]],
['Radiation, laser, chemical and sharps safety',[
'**Radiation** from fluoroscopy and X-rays is reduced by **time** (minimize exposure), **distance** (stand at least 6 feet away when possible) and **shielding** (lead aprons, thyroid shields and glasses); staff wear **dosimeter badges**. **Lasers** require eye protection matched to the laser wavelength, warning signs on doors, laser-safe masks and non-reflective instruments. **Chemical hazards** include **methyl methacrylate** (bone cement fumes), **formalin** (specimen preservative), **glutaraldehyde** and anesthetic gases; follow Safety Data Sheets and use ventilation and PPE.',
'**Sharps safety** follows OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard: use a **neutral zone** (a designated area where sharps are placed and picked up instead of hand-to-hand passing), **double gloving**, safety scalpels, instruments rather than fingers to handle needles and puncture-resistant sharps containers. **Latex allergy** precautions and **ergonomics** (anti-fatigue mats, proper table height, avoiding twisting) protect staff.',
match('Hazard and protection',[['Fluoroscopy','Time, distance and lead shielding'],['Laser','Wavelength-specific eye protection and door signs'],['Methyl methacrylate','Ventilation and fume control'],['Formalin','Safety Data Sheet precautions and PPE'],['Scalpel passing','Neutral zone'],['Long standing cases','Anti-fatigue mats and good posture']])]],
['Patient positioning',[
'Positioning provides surgical exposure while protecting the airway, circulation, nerves and skin. Common positions: **supine** (most abdominal procedures), **Trendelenburg** (head down, for pelvic and lower abdominal surgery), **reverse Trendelenburg** (head up, for upper abdominal and laparoscopic gallbladder surgery), **lithotomy** (legs in stirrups, for vaginal, perineal and urologic procedures—raise and lower the legs **slowly and together**), **prone** (spine and posterior procedures, with chest rolls to allow breathing), **lateral** (kidney and chest procedures, with an axillary roll), **Fowler’s/beach chair** (shoulder and some neurosurgery) and **Kraske (jackknife)** for rectal surgery.',
'Positioning injuries include **pressure injuries** over bony prominences, **nerve injuries** (ulnar nerve at the elbow; brachial plexus if the arm is abducted more than 90°; peroneal nerve at the lateral knee in lithotomy), **corneal abrasions** and circulatory problems. Use padding, safety straps and enough staff to move anesthetized patients, and keep arms on armboards at **less than 90°** of abduction.',
sort('Which position fits?',['Supine','Lithotomy','Prone','Lateral'],[['Laparoscopic appendectomy',0],['Vaginal hysterectomy',1],['Lumbar spine surgery',2],['Nephrectomy',3],['Cystoscopy',1],['Thoracotomy',3]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Fire risk',{a:{text:'A patient having a mole removed from the face is receiving oxygen by nasal cannula. The skin was prepped with an alcohol-based solution a moment ago. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should happen before the ESU is used?',choices:[['Let the prep dry completely and ask anesthesia to lower or stop supplemental oxygen as safely possible','b','Correct. Reduce fuel and oxidizer near an ignition source.',true],['Use the ESU right away to save time','b','Wet alcohol and oxygen create a fire risk.',false]]},b:{text:'A small flame appears on the drape. What is the first action?',choices:[['Stop the flow of oxygen and remove the burning drape','c','Correct. Remove the oxidizer and the fuel.',true],['Keep operating','c','Act immediately.',false]]},c:{text:'The fire is extinguished with saline, the patient is assessed for burns and the event is reported.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Zones: unrestricted (street clothes), semirestricted (scrubs and hair covered), restricted (plus masks); positive pressure, ≥20 air exchanges per hour, about 68–75 °F and 20–60% humidity.','Monopolar ESU needs a return pad on clean, dry skin over a large muscle mass away from bone, scars and metal; bipolar does not; holster the pencil; the fire triangle is ignition, fuel and oxidizer—let preps dry and limit oxygen.','Radiation safety is time, distance and shielding; use laser eye protection, the neutral zone and double gloving; position safely with padding and arms under 90°.'],terms:[['Patient return electrode','A pad that safely returns monopolar current to the ESU.'],['Neutral zone','A designated area for placing sharps instead of passing hand to hand.'],['Restricted zone','OR areas where masks are required with sterile supplies open.']],mcqs:[q('Which OR zone requires masks when sterile supplies are open?','Restricted','Unrestricted','Semirestricted','Locker room','Operating rooms.'),q('ORs are kept at what air pressure relative to hallways?','Positive','Negative','Equal','Variable','Air flows out.'),q('Where should a monopolar return electrode be placed?','Over a large, well-perfused muscle mass','Over a bony prominence','Over a metal implant','On scar tissue','Prevents burns.'),q('Which is an oxidizer in the fire triangle?','Oxygen','Drapes','ESU','Alcohol prep','Also nitrous oxide.'),q('Which electrosurgery type needs no return pad?','Bipolar','Monopolar','Both','Neither','Current between forceps tips.'),q('The three principles of radiation safety are:','Time, distance and shielding','Speed, power and heat','Gloves, gown and mask','Light, sound and air','ALARA approach.'),q('Lithotomy legs should be raised:','Slowly and together','One at a time quickly','Only the right leg','By the patient','Prevents hip and nerve injury.'),q('Arms on armboards should be abducted less than:','90°','45°','180°','120°','Protects the brachial plexus.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how the OR team prevents surgical fires and electrosurgical burns during a procedure on the neck in a patient receiving supplemental oxygen.',rubric:[['Fire triangle',['ignition','fuel','oxidizer','esu','drapes','alcohol','oxygen']],['Fire prevention',['dry','3 minutes','pooling','lowest oxygen','moist sponges','holster','light cord']],['ESU burn prevention',['return electrode','muscle mass','bony','metal','dry skin','close to site','bipolar']],['Response',['stop gases','remove','extinguish','saline','assess','report']]],model:'A neck procedure in a patient on supplemental oxygen combines all three parts of the fire triangle: ignition sources such as the electrosurgical pencil, fuels such as drapes, sponges, hair and alcohol-based prep, and an oxidizer—oxygen pooling under the drapes. To prevent fire, the team lets the alcohol-based prep dry completely, usually at least three minutes, and prevents pooling; the anesthesia provider uses the lowest oxygen concentration that keeps the patient safe, or stops it briefly before the ESU is used near the airway; drapes are arranged so oxygen can vent; sponges near the field are moistened; the ESU pencil is kept in its insulated holster when not in use; and light cords are kept off drapes. To prevent electrosurgical burns, the patient return electrode is placed on clean, dry, hairless skin over a large, well-perfused muscle mass close to the site, away from bony prominences, scars and metal implants, and the lowest effective settings are used; bipolar electrosurgery may be chosen for delicate areas. If a fire occurs, the team stops the flow of gases, removes burning materials, extinguishes the fire with saline, checks the patient for burns and airway injury and reports the event.'},sources:[RULES,FDA_FIRE,OSHA_BBP,AST]};

export const G3={title:'Surgical asepsis and the surgical scrub (Skill II)',hook:'Surgical site infections are among the most common infections acquired in hospitals, and every one starts with microorganisms reaching the wound. Surgical asepsis—the rules that keep the sterile field free of microorganisms—is the core of the surgical technologist’s job and 20% of the test. The surgical scrub is the first step every time you join the sterile team.',objectives:['Explain the difference between medical and surgical asepsis and the sources of surgical site infection.','Apply the principles of sterile technique to the sterile field.','Perform the counted brush-stroke or timed surgical scrub following HOSA Skill II.','Enter the OR and approach the sterile field correctly after scrubbing.'],sections:[
['Asepsis and surgical site infections',[
'**Medical asepsis** (clean technique) reduces the number of microorganisms; **surgical asepsis** (sterile technique) keeps an area **free of all microorganisms**. **Surgical site infections (SSIs)** come mostly from the patient’s own skin flora, but also from the team, instruments, the environment and the air. Risk factors include obesity, diabetes, smoking, poor nutrition, long procedures, wound contamination and breaks in technique.',
'Prevention measures include appropriate **antibiotic prophylaxis** within an hour before incision (as ordered), **skin antisepsis**, hair removal only when necessary using **clippers** (not razors), maintaining normal body temperature and glucose, limiting OR traffic, proper attire and strict aseptic technique. The CST’s **surgical conscience** is the last line of defense.',
"Attire is part of asepsis: facility-laundered **scrub attire**, **head covering** that contains all hair, a **mask** covering the nose and mouth in restricted areas, **eye protection** and clean shoes or shoe covers. Jewelry and artificial nails harbor microorganisms and are not worn. Scrub attire worn outside the facility should be changed before re-entering the semirestricted zone.",
sort('Medical or surgical asepsis?',['Medical asepsis (clean)','Surgical asepsis (sterile)'],[['Handwashing before lunch',0],['Setting up the back table',1],['Cleaning a counter with disinfectant',0],['Sterile gloving for surgery',1],['Covering a cough',0],['Opening a sterile instrument tray onto the field',1]])]],
['Principles of sterile technique',[
'Key rules: **only sterile items touch sterile items**; **sterile persons touch only sterile items** and non-sterile persons touch only non-sterile items; a **sterile gown** is considered sterile in front **from the chest to the level of the sterile field** and the **sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff**—the back, neckline, axillae and below the table level are not sterile; **tables are sterile only at table level**; the **edges of a sterile wrapper** (about 1 inch, or the 2-inch perimeter described in HOSA’s gloving sheet) are considered non-sterile.',
'More rules: keep hands **above the waist and in sight**; sterile persons pass each other **back to back or front to front**; non-sterile persons stay at least **12 inches** (1 foot) from the field and never reach over it; **moisture** can carry microorganisms through a package (**strike-through**), so wet packages are considered contaminated; the field is prepared as close to the time of use as possible and **never left unattended**; and **when in doubt, throw it out**. Any break is reported and corrected immediately.',
flip('Sterile field rules',[['Gown sterile area','Front from chest to field level; sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to cuff'],['Table','Sterile only at table level'],['Wrapper edges','Considered non-sterile'],['Passing','Back to back or front to front'],['Non-sterile distance','At least 12 inches from the field'],['Strike-through','Moisture wicking microorganisms through a package']])]],
['The surgical scrub: preparation (steps 1–10)',[
'Skill II has 15 steps in **6 minutes**. **1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Don all PPE—cap, mask, eye protection, shoe covers—with the **scrub top tucked in** and **sleeves rolled up 2 inches above the elbows**. **3.** Remove all **jewelry**. **4.** Inspect hands and arms for **non-intact skin** (cuts or rashes mean you should not scrub). **5.** Inspect **nails and cuticles** (short, natural nails; no artificial nails or polish). **6.** Open the brush package and place it conveniently.',
'**7.** Turn on the water, **wet hands and arms** and lather to **2 inches above the elbows**. **8.** Use the **nail cleaner** to clean the **subungual areas** (under the nails) of both hands under running water. **9.** Discard the nail cleaner properly. **10.** Unwrap the scrub sponge and dispense soap unless the sponge is already **impregnated** with antiseptic. Throughout, keep **hands higher than elbows** so water runs from the cleanest area (hands) toward the less clean area (elbows).',
order('Scrub preparation',['Assemble equipment','PPE on; scrub top tucked in; sleeves 2 inches above the elbows','Remove jewelry','Inspect skin, nails and cuticles','Open the brush package','Wet and lather hands and arms to 2 inches above the elbows','Clean under the nails with the nail cleaner under running water','Discard the cleaner; prepare the sponge'])]],
['The scrub itself and rinsing (steps 11–14)',[
'**11.** Start **timing** or **counting strokes**—HOSA’s sheet allows about **2 minutes per arm** (many facilities use a total scrub time recommended by the antiseptic manufacturer). **12.** Begin with the **nails**, passing the fingertips back and forth across the brush. Keep **hands above elbows** and **elbows bent**. Scrub each **finger as having four planes** (palm side, back and two sides), including the **web spaces** between fingers. Then divide the **arm into three sections**, each with four planes, up to **2 inches above the elbow**, scrubbing from hand toward elbow and never going back to an area already scrubbed.',
'**13.** **Transfer the brush** to the scrubbed hand and repeat the process on the other extremity, then discard the brush. **14.** **Rinse** one extremity from **fingertips to elbow** in one direction, keeping elbows bent and hands above elbows, without touching your body, the faucet or the sink; repeat on the other arm. If your hands touch anything non-sterile, the scrub must be repeated.',
order('Scrub sequence for each arm',['Start timing or counting strokes','Scrub the nails','Scrub four planes of each finger and the web spaces','Scrub the hand','Scrub the arm in three sections with four planes up to 2 inches above the elbow','Transfer the brush and repeat on the other arm','Rinse fingertips to elbow, hands above elbows'])]],
['Entering the OR (step 15)',[
'**15.** Proceed to the OR with **elbows bent** and **hands between mid-chest and waist level**, in front of you. **a.** Enter by **turning your back to the door, pushing it open with your back** and rotating into the room. **b.** Do not touch anything—including your scrub suit or the other arm—and do not cross your arms until you are gowned and gloved. **c.** Keep elbows flexed and proceed to **gown and glove** (the judge then says “Skill completed”). Water should drip from the elbows, not the hands.',
'Some facilities use **brushless, alcohol-based surgical hand antisepsis** after an initial wash; follow the product and facility protocol. In the HOSA event, follow the brush-and-sponge steps on the rating sheet exactly.',
chk('How should you enter the OR after scrubbing?',['Back first, pushing the door open with your back, hands up and in front','Push the door with your hands','Walk in with arms crossed','Dry your hands on your scrubs first'],'Hands stay clean between mid-chest and waist level.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Scrub breaks',{a:{text:'During your scrub, your fingertips brush the faucet. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Start the scrub over','b','Correct. Contaminated hands must be rescrubbed.',true],['Keep going—it was only a touch','b','Any contact with non-sterile surfaces breaks the scrub.',false]]},b:{text:'You finish rinsing. Which way should water drain?',choices:[['From the fingertips toward the elbows','c','Correct. Hands stay higher than elbows.',true],['From the elbows toward the fingertips','c','That carries less-clean water onto the hands.',false]]},c:{text:'You back through the door with hands between mid-chest and waist and proceed to gown and glove.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Surgical asepsis keeps an area free of all microorganisms; most SSIs come from the patient’s skin flora; clippers, antibiotics as ordered, warmth, glucose control and strict technique prevent them.','Sterile rules: sterile touches sterile; gowns sterile in front from chest to field level and sleeves 2 inches above the elbow to cuff; tables sterile at table level; wrapper edges non-sterile; strike-through contaminates; when in doubt, throw it out.','Skill II: PPE with scrub top tucked and sleeves 2 inches above the elbows, jewelry off, inspect skin and nails, nail cleaner, scrub nails, four planes of each finger and web spaces, arm in three sections to 2 inches above the elbow (about 2 minutes per arm), rinse fingertips to elbows, back into the OR with hands up.'],terms:[['Surgical asepsis','Techniques that keep an area free of all microorganisms.'],['Strike-through','Contamination when moisture wicks microorganisms through a sterile package.'],['Subungual area','The space under the fingernails.']],mcqs:[q('Contamination caused by microorganisms passing through a sterilized package by moisture wicking is termed:','Strike-through','Saturation','Solubilization','Sequestration','HOSA sample question.'),q('A sterile gown is considered sterile:','In front from chest to the level of the sterile field','Everywhere','Only on the back','From the neck to the floor','Plus sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff.'),q('During the scrub, hands are kept:','Above the elbows','Below the elbows','At the sides','Behind the back','Water runs toward the elbows.'),q('How many planes does each finger have in the scrub?','Four','Two','Six','One','Palm, back and two sides.'),q('How long does HOSA’s sheet allow per arm?','About 2 minutes','10 seconds','10 minutes','30 seconds','Timed or counted strokes.'),q('How far above the elbows do you scrub?','2 inches','6 inches','To the shoulder','Only to the wrist','Per Skill II.'),q('How do you enter the OR after scrubbing?','Back first, pushing the door with your back','Hands first','Sideways with arms crossed','Through a curtain with your hands','Keep hands clean.'),q('Hair removal before surgery, when needed, should use:','Clippers','A razor','Depilatory cream only','Scissors on the field','Razors increase SSI risk.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to perform the surgical scrub following HOSA Skill II, from preparation through entering the operating room, and explain why hands are kept above the elbows.',rubric:[['Preparation',['ppe','tucked','sleeves','2 inches','jewelry','skin','nails','cuticles']],['Nail and lather',['wet','lather','nail cleaner','subungual','running water','sponge','soap']],['Scrub technique',['timing','strokes','2 minutes','nails','four planes','web spaces','three sections','transfer']],['Rinse and entry',['rinse','fingertips to elbow','elbows bent','mid-chest','waist','back','door','gown and glove','hands above elbows']]],model:'I assemble my equipment and put on my cap, mask, eye protection and shoe covers, tuck in my scrub top and roll my sleeves 2 inches above my elbows. I remove all jewelry and inspect my hands and arms for cuts or rashes and my nails and cuticles. I open the brush package and place it nearby, turn on the water, wet my hands and arms and lather to 2 inches above my elbows. Holding my hands under running water, I use the nail cleaner to clean under every nail, then discard it and unwrap the scrub sponge, dispensing soap unless it is impregnated. I start timing or counting strokes, about 2 minutes per arm. I scrub my nails first, then scrub each finger as having four planes, including the web spaces, then the hand, and then the arm divided into three sections with four planes each up to 2 inches above the elbow, always moving from hand toward elbow. I transfer the brush to my scrubbed hand, repeat on the other arm and discard the brush. I rinse each arm from fingertips to elbow without touching my body, the faucet or the sink. Hands are kept above the elbows and the elbows bent throughout so water flows from the cleanest area, the hands, toward the less clean elbows and never back onto the hands. I walk to the OR with elbows bent and hands between mid-chest and waist, push the door open with my back, touch nothing and proceed to gown and glove.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_SSI,AST]};

export const G4={title:'Decontamination, sterilization and open gloving (Skill III)',hook:'A tray of instruments used on one patient may be used on another just hours later. Between those cases, the instruments are cleaned, inspected, packaged and sterilized—and every step must be done correctly, or the next patient is at risk. This lesson covers sterile processing and the open gloving technique used when you need sterile gloves without a sterile gown.',objectives:['Describe the instrument-processing cycle from point-of-use cleaning to sterile storage.','Compare steam, ethylene oxide, hydrogen peroxide gas plasma and other sterilization methods.','Interpret chemical, mechanical and biological indicators and event-related sterility.','Perform open gloving following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['The processing cycle and Spaulding classification',[
'Instruments move through a cycle: **point-of-use cleaning** (wiping blood and debris off instruments during surgery with sterile water and keeping them moist), **transport** in closed containers, **decontamination** (manual cleaning, ultrasonic cleaners that remove debris from crevices and washer-disinfectors), **inspection and assembly** (checking function, alignment and cleanliness), **packaging**, **sterilization**, **storage** and **distribution**. Instruments must be **clean before sterilization**, because blood and tissue protect microorganisms.',
'The **Spaulding classification** guides processing: **critical** items enter sterile tissue or the vascular system (surgical instruments, implants) and must be **sterilized**; **semicritical** items contact mucous membranes (some endoscopes) and need at least **high-level disinfection**; **noncritical** items contact intact skin (blood pressure cuffs) and need **low-level disinfection**.',
order('Instrument processing cycle',['Point-of-use cleaning','Transport in closed containers','Decontamination and cleaning','Inspection and assembly','Packaging','Sterilization','Sterile storage and distribution'])]],
['Sterilization methods',[
'**Steam sterilization** (the autoclave) is the most common, inexpensive and reliable method for heat- and moisture-tolerant items. **Gravity displacement** sterilizers typically run at **121 °C (250 °F)** for about 15–30 minutes; **dynamic air removal (prevacuum)** sterilizers run at **132–135 °C (270–275 °F)** for about **3–4 minutes** of exposure, plus drying. **Immediate-use steam sterilization** (formerly “flash”) is reserved for urgent situations, not for convenience or implants.',
'Low-temperature methods protect heat-sensitive items: **ethylene oxide (EO)** gas penetrates well but is toxic and requires long **aeration**; **hydrogen peroxide gas plasma** is fast with nontoxic by-products but cannot process liquids, powders or linens; **peracetic acid** liquid systems are used for some endoscopes; **dry heat** suits powders and oils. Sterilized packs are wrapped in woven or non-woven wraps, peel pouches or rigid containers.',
match('Sterilization method',[['Steam (autoclave)','Most common; heat- and moisture-tolerant items'],['Prevacuum steam','132–135 °C for about 3–4 minutes'],['Ethylene oxide','Low temperature; requires aeration'],['Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma','Low temperature; no linens or liquids'],['Immediate-use steam sterilization','Urgent situations only'],['Dry heat','Powders and oils']])]],
['Indicators and sterile storage',[
'**Mechanical indicators** (gauges and printouts of time, temperature and pressure), **chemical indicators** (tape and strips that **change color** when exposed to certain conditions) and **biological indicators** together monitor sterilization. Chemical indicators show **exposure**, **not sterility**. **Biological indicators** contain highly resistant spores—***Geobacillus stearothermophilus*** for steam and hydrogen peroxide, ***Bacillus atrophaeus*** for EO and dry heat—and are the best proof that sterilization conditions killed microorganisms.',
'Most facilities use **event-related sterility**: a package remains sterile until an **event** contaminates it—**moisture**, tears, punctures, compression, dropping on the floor or a broken seal—rather than until a set expiration date. Store sterile items in a clean, dry, controlled area off the floor, away from sinks and outside walls, and rotate stock (first in, first out). Before opening, check the package integrity, the chemical indicator and any expiration date.',
"Sterile processing also tracks **load records** so items can be **recalled** if a biological indicator fails, and implants are not used until biological indicator results are known whenever possible. Quality depends on every link: a perfectly run sterilizer cannot fix an instrument that was not cleaned.",
sort('Is the package usable?',['Usable','Do not use'],[['Dry, intact wrap with changed indicator tape',0],['Wrap with a water stain',1],['Pouch with a broken seal',1],['Package dropped on the floor',1],['Rigid container with intact locks and filters',0],['Indicator tape did not change color',1]])]],
['Open gloving: Skill III',[
'**Open gloving** is used when sterile gloves are needed **without a sterile gown**—for example, for a sterile procedure at the bedside or when gloving to prep. Skill III has 11 steps in **3 minutes**. **1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Remove jewelry. **3.** Hand hygiene. **4.** Open the **outer non-sterile wrapper** and deliver the inner sterile-wrapped gloves onto a **clean, dry surface**. **5.** Open the inner wrapper to create a small sterile field—**the outer 2 inches** are considered non-sterile—touching only the outer edges of the inside wrapper.',
'**5b.** Pick up the **first glove by pinching the folded cuff** (touching only the inside) and lift it up and away from the wrapper. **5c.** Point the **fingertips downward**. **6.** Insert the opposite hand and pull the glove over the palm **without touching the glove’s exterior**. **7.** Unfold the cuff without touching the exterior. **8.** Pick up the second glove by sliding the **fingertips of the gloved hand under its cuff**. **9.** Keep the **gloved thumb tucked** under the cuff or out of the way. **10.** Lift the glove up and away. **11.** Apply it the same way, making minor adjustments **without touching the non-sterile hand** (gloved touches gloved only).',
order('Open gloving',['Open the outer wrapper; place the inner wrap on a clean, dry surface','Open the inner wrapper touching only its outer edges','Pinch the first glove’s folded cuff; lift up and away','Fingertips down; slide the hand in without touching the outside','Gloved fingers under the second glove’s cuff; thumb tucked','Lift away and pull on','Adjust gloved hand to gloved hand only'])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Sterile processing judgment',{a:{text:'You pick up a sterile instrument pack and notice a small wet spot on the wrapper. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Consider it contaminated and do not use it','b','Correct. Moisture can cause strike-through.',true],['Dry it with a towel and use it','b','Strike-through may already have occurred.',false]]},b:{text:'A sterile tray’s internal chemical indicator did not change color. What next?',choices:[['Do not use it; report it and get another tray','c','Correct. The tray may not have been exposed to sterilant.',true],['Use it because the outer tape changed','c','The internal indicator shows conditions inside the tray.',false]]},c:{text:'You report the failed indicator to sterile processing, which investigates the load.',end:true}})]],
["Packaging and opening sterile items",["Items are packaged so they can be **opened without contamination**. **Wrapped packs** use the envelope or square fold, sealed with chemical indicator tape and labeled with contents, date, sterilizer and load number. **Peel pouches** are sealed on all sides; the item is presented by peeling back the edges and letting the sterile person take it, never “flipping” heavy items. **Rigid containers** have filters and tamper-evident locks.","When the circulator opens a wrapped pack, the **first flap is opened away** from the body, then the sides, and the last flap **toward** the body, so the person never reaches across the sterile inside. Sterile items are delivered to the field by **presenting** them to the scrubbed person or placing them on the table—not tossing them from a distance. Solutions are poured slowly into a labeled container at the edge of the table without splashing.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Opening a wrapped pack (non-sterile person)","steps":["Check package integrity and indicator","Open the first flap away from your body","Open the side flaps","Open the last flap toward your body","Present the contents to the sterile person or field","Never reach across the sterile inside"]}]]
],takeaways:['Processing: point-of-use cleaning, decontamination (ultrasonic, washer), inspection, packaging, sterilization, storage; Spaulding: critical items are sterilized, semicritical get high-level disinfection, noncritical get low-level.','Steam is most common (gravity ~121 °C; prevacuum 132–135 °C for ~3–4 minutes); EO needs aeration; hydrogen peroxide plasma cannot do linens or liquids; chemical indicators show exposure, biological indicators (Geobacillus stearothermophilus for steam) show kill.','Event-related sterility: moisture, tears, drops and broken seals contaminate; Skill III: outer wrapper off, inner wrapper opened by its edges (2-inch border non-sterile), pinch the first cuff, fingertips down, gloved fingers under the second cuff with thumb tucked, gloved touches gloved only.'],terms:[['Biological indicator','A spore test that proves sterilization conditions killed resistant organisms.'],['Event-related sterility','A package stays sterile until an event such as moisture or a tear contaminates it.'],['Open gloving','Donning sterile gloves without a sterile gown.']],mcqs:[q('Which sterilization method is most common in hospitals?','Steam','Ethylene oxide','Dry heat','Radiation','Autoclave.'),q('Which spore is used to test steam sterilization?','Geobacillus stearothermophilus','Bacillus atrophaeus','E. coli','Clostridium difficile','Biological indicator.'),q('A chemical indicator that changed color shows:','Exposure to sterilization conditions','Guaranteed sterility','Contamination','That the item is expired','Not proof of sterility.'),q('Which method requires aeration afterward?','Ethylene oxide','Steam','Hydrogen peroxide gas plasma','Dry heat','Toxic gas residue.'),q('Critical items in the Spaulding classification must be:','Sterilized','Wiped with soap','Low-level disinfected','Rinsed with water','Enter sterile tissue.'),q('In open gloving, how is the first glove picked up?','By pinching the folded cuff','By the fingers','By the palm','With a gloved hand','Touch only the inside.'),q('How is the second glove picked up?','Gloved fingers slide under the cuff','By the inside of the cuff with a bare hand','By the fingertips','By the wrapper only','Gloved touches the outside.'),q('Which event makes a sterile package unusable?','It becomes wet','It is stored on a shelf','It is older than one week','It has a label','Event-related sterility.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how surgical instruments are processed from the end of a case until they are ready for the next patient, including how sterilization is verified and how event-related sterility applies in storage.',rubric:[['Point of use and decontamination',['point-of-use','moist','transport','closed','decontamination','ultrasonic','washer']],['Inspection and packaging',['inspect','function','assemble','package','wrap','pouch','container']],['Sterilization and verification',['steam','ethylene oxide','hydrogen peroxide','mechanical','chemical','biological','geobacillus']],['Storage and checks',['event-related','moisture','tear','dropped','seal','first in','check indicator']]],model:'At the end of a case, the CST wipes gross blood and debris off the instruments with sterile water, keeps them moist, opens box locks and places sharps safely, and the instruments travel to sterile processing in a closed container. In decontamination, technicians wearing PPE clean them manually and in ultrasonic cleaners and washer-disinfectors so no bioburden remains, because dirt protects microorganisms. Each instrument is then inspected for cleanliness, alignment and function, assembled on trays with internal chemical indicators and packaged in wraps, peel pouches or rigid containers. Most instruments are steam sterilized—for example, prevacuum cycles at 132–135 °C for about 3–4 minutes—while heat-sensitive items go through ethylene oxide with aeration or hydrogen peroxide gas plasma. Sterilization is verified with mechanical indicators such as printouts of time, temperature and pressure, chemical indicators that change color to show exposure and biological indicators containing Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores for steam, which prove the cycle killed resistant organisms. In storage, event-related sterility means a package stays sterile until something contaminates it—moisture, tears, punctures, crushing, dropping or a broken seal—so packs are stored clean, dry and off the floor, rotated first in, first out, and checked for integrity and indicator change before opening.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_STERIL,AST]};

export const G5={title:'Gowning and gloving, the sterile field and draping (Skill IV)',hook:'After the scrub, the CST must put on a sterile gown and gloves without touching anything non-sterile—using only the inside of the gown and the gown’s cuffs. Then comes setting up the back table and Mayo stand, gowning the surgeon and draping the patient. Skill IV tests self-gowning and closed gloving in 5 minutes.',objectives:['Gown and glove yourself with the closed technique following HOSA Skill IV.','Explain double gloving and how to check gloves.','Set up and maintain the back table and Mayo stand.','Describe skin preparation, draping and gowning and gloving others.'],sections:[
['Donning the gown: Skill IV steps 1–3',[
'Skill IV has 8 steps in **5 minutes**. **1.** Approach the sterile field **with caution**. **2a.** Grasp the gown at the **center neck** without touching the glove or the wrapper. **b.** Lift the gown from the wrapper and **step back** with the gown still folded, away from the table. **c.** Holding the **inside of the gown near the shoulders**, let it **unfold**—**do not shake it**. **d.** Slip your hands and arms into the sleeves, sliding the gown over the arms with a **“swimming” motion**.',
'**3.** Keep your **hands within the cuffs** of the gown and **flex your elbows** to hold the gown in place. The **circulator** then reaches inside the back of the gown to pull it onto your shoulders and ties the neck and inner waist ties. Because your hands never leave the cuffs, you can glove using the **closed technique**, which keeps bare skin from touching the gloves’ exterior.',
order('Gown donning',['Approach the field with caution','Grasp the gown at the center neck','Lift it and step back','Hold the inside at the shoulders and let it unfold—no shaking','Slide arms into the sleeves with a swimming motion','Keep hands inside the cuffs; elbows flexed'])]],
['Closed gloving: Skill IV steps 4–8',[
'**4a.** Begin **closed gloving** without letting fingers protrude from the knitted cuffs. **b.** Approach the field with caution. **c.** Open the glove wrapper by grasping the two center edges through the gown sleeves and opening outward, everting the package slightly. **d.** Pick up the first glove through the sleeve. **e.** Lay the glove on the **palm of the hand to be gloved**, with the **thumb side of the glove toward your palm** and the **fingertips pointing toward your elbow**. **f.** Pull the glove’s cuff over the gown’s cuff. **g.** Unfold the glove cuff to cover the gown cuff completely. **h.** Grasp the glove and gown at the wrist and **work the fingers into the glove** as it is pulled into position.',
'**5.** Apply the second glove the same way with the other hand. **6.** **Check both gloves** for punctures or tears. **7.** Repeat the process to **double glove**—double gloving reduces exposure to blood when the outer glove is perforated. **8.** Pull the tie to release it from the **paper tab**, **pass the tab to the circulator** (the judge), **turn** to wrap the gown ties around you and take the tie from the tab to secure the gown. Now the back of the gown is covered, but it is still considered non-sterile.',
order('Closed gloving',['Keep fingers inside the cuffs','Open the glove wrapper through the sleeves','Place the glove palm-side, thumb toward your palm, fingers toward the elbow','Pull the glove cuff over the gown cuff','Work the fingers in while pulling the glove on','Glove the second hand','Check for tears; double glove','Pass the tie tab to the circulator, turn and tie'])]],
['Setting up the field',[
'The **back table** holds most instruments and supplies; the **Mayo stand** holds the instruments used most frequently and is positioned over the patient. Setup follows a consistent pattern: drape the back table and Mayo stand, organize instruments by use (sharps in a defined area), prepare **basins** with sterile water or saline, **label all medications and solutions** on and off the field (name, strength, amount), arrange **sponges** for counting and prepare sutures in the order the surgeon will use them.',
'Medications are received onto the field with the circulator verifying the drug, dose and expiration aloud, and the CST **repeats and labels** each one immediately. Unlabeled medications on the field are discarded. The field is never left unattended once opened.',
flip('Field setup facts',[['Back table','Holds most instruments and supplies'],['Mayo stand','Most-used instruments over the patient'],['Medications','Verified aloud and labeled immediately'],['Unlabeled solution','Discard it'],['Basins','Sterile water or saline'],['Opened field','Never left unattended']])]],
['Skin prep and draping',[
'The **skin prep** reduces skin microorganisms at the incision site. Common antiseptics include **chlorhexidine gluconate with alcohol** and **povidone-iodine**; the prep usually starts at the **incision site** and moves **outward** in a circular or back-and-forth pattern (contaminated areas such as the umbilicus or stoma are cleaned separately or last). Alcohol-based preps must dry completely to prevent fire. **Drapes** create a sterile barrier around the incision: the CST hands drapes to the surgeon, holding them high and keeping the **cuffed hands protected** under the drape’s fold. Once placed, drapes are **not moved toward** the incision.',
'The CST also **gowns and gloves the surgeon** and assistant: hand the gown folded inside-out so the surgeon can slide arms in, then hold each glove open with the palm toward the surgeon, stretching the cuff so the surgeon’s hand enters without touching the CST’s gloves. The circulator ties the back.',
sort('Correct or incorrect draping and prep?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Prep from the incision site outward',0],['Moving a placed drape closer to the incision',1],['Protecting gloved hands in the drape’s cuff',0],['Letting an alcohol prep dry before draping',0],['Reaching over a non-sterile area to drape',1],['Discarding a drape that falls below the table',0]])]],
['Removing gown and gloves',[
'At the end of a case, the **gown is removed first**, then the gloves, so contaminated gown sleeves do not touch bare skin: the circulator unties the back, the CST pulls the gown forward and off by the shoulders, turning it inside out, and then removes the gloves using a **glove-to-glove, skin-to-skin** technique. Perform hand hygiene afterward. If a glove is contaminated during surgery, the circulator removes it, and the CST regloves using the **assisted** or **open** technique (closed gloving is used only when first gowning).',
kase('Gowning decisions',{a:{text:'While unfolding your gown, the bottom touches the floor. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Discard it and get a new sterile gown','b','Correct. It is contaminated.',true],['Shake it off and put it on','b','Shaking spreads contamination; the gown is no longer sterile.',false]]},b:{text:'During closed gloving, your fingers poke out of the cuff. What now?',choices:[['Consider the cuff contaminated and change technique per policy (for example, have the gloves applied by another sterile person)','c','Correct. Bare fingers outside the cuff break closed technique.',true],['Continue as normal','c','The cuff is now contaminated.',false]]},c:{text:'You report the break, correct it and continue with surgical conscience.',end:true}})]],
["Assisted gloving and maintaining the field",["The CST often **gowns and gloves other team members**. To glove the surgeon, the CST picks up a glove, holds it with the palm facing the surgeon and stretches the cuff open with fingers under the cuff (thumbs out of the way); the surgeon slides a hand in while the CST pulls the cuff over the gown cuff. If a team member’s glove becomes contaminated during surgery, the circulator removes it and the CST **regloves** the person using this assisted technique.","During the case, keep the field **organized and dry**, keep sharps contained, never turn your back on the field and stay with it once it is open. If the patient’s position or the drapes shift, or a team member’s gown sleeve touches a non-sterile area, announce it and correct it. Wet drapes allow strike-through, so additional sterile drapes may be placed over wet areas.",{"t":"check","q":"A team member’s glove is punctured during surgery. Which technique is used to reglove?","options":["Assisted gloving by the CST after the circulator removes the glove","Closed gloving","Open gloving by the surgeon alone","No new glove is needed"],"why":"Closed gloving is used only during initial gowning."}]]
],takeaways:['Skill IV gown: approach with caution, grasp at the center neck, step back, hold the inside at the shoulders and let it unfold without shaking, swim arms in, keep hands in the cuffs.','Closed gloving: glove on the palm, thumb side toward the palm and fingers toward the elbow, pull the glove cuff over the gown cuff, work the fingers in, check for tears, double glove, pass the tie tab to the circulator and turn.','Set up the back table and Mayo stand, label every medication, prep from the incision outward and let alcohol dry, drape without moving drapes toward the incision, and remove the gown before the gloves.'],terms:[['Closed gloving','Gloving with the hands kept inside the gown cuffs.'],['Mayo stand','A tray positioned over the patient for frequently used instruments.'],['Double gloving','Wearing two pairs of sterile gloves to reduce exposure.']],mcqs:[q('Where do you grasp the gown first?','At the center neck','At the hem','By the sleeves','By the back ties','Avoid touching the wrapper.'),q('How should the gown be unfolded?','Let it unfold while holding the inside at the shoulders—no shaking','Shake it open','Throw it in the air','Unfold it on the floor','Shaking spreads contamination.'),q('In closed gloving, the glove’s fingers point:','Toward your elbow','Toward your fingertips','Upward','Toward the floor','Thumb side toward your palm.'),q('Why double glove?','To reduce blood exposure if the outer glove is perforated','For warmth','To look professional','It is not recommended','Recommended for surgery.'),q('Who receives the gown tie tab?','The circulator','The surgeon','The anesthesia provider','Nobody','Then you turn.'),q('Which is removed first at the end of a case?','The gown','The gloves','The mask','The cap','Then the gloves.'),q('Skin prep usually moves:','From the incision site outward','From the outside inward','In random directions','Only downward','Clean to dirty.'),q('A solution on the field has no label. What should be done?','Discard it','Use it if it looks like saline','Ask the patient','Pour it back','Label everything.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to gown and glove yourself following HOSA Skill IV, including closed gloving and double gloving, and how you would maintain sterility afterward.',rubric:[['Gowning',['caution','center neck','step back','inside','shoulders','unfold','not shake','swimming','cuffs']],['Closed gloving',['fingers inside','palm','thumb','elbow','cuff over','work fingers']],['Double gloving and ties',['check','punctures','tears','double','tab','circulator','turn']],['Maintaining sterility',['hands above waist','in sight','chest to field','back non-sterile','report break','surgical conscience']]],model:'I approach the sterile field with caution, grasp the gown at the center neck without touching the wrapper, lift it and step back with it still folded. Holding the inside near the shoulders, I let it unfold without shaking, then slide my arms into the sleeves with a swimming motion, keeping my hands inside the cuffs and my elbows flexed while the circulator pulls the gown onto my shoulders and ties it. For closed gloving, I keep my fingers inside the knitted cuffs, open the glove wrapper through my sleeves and lay the first glove on the palm of the hand to be gloved with the thumb side toward my palm and the fingertips toward my elbow. I pull the glove cuff over the gown cuff, unfold it to cover the gown cuff completely, then grasp the glove and gown at the wrist and work my fingers into the glove. I repeat with the other hand, check both gloves for punctures or tears and add a second pair to double glove, which reduces blood exposure if the outer glove is punctured. I pull the tie from its paper tab, pass the tab to the circulator, turn and take back the tie to secure the gown. Afterward, I keep my hands above my waist and in sight, remember that only the front from chest to field level and the sleeves are sterile, never turn my back to the field and report and correct any break in sterility immediately.'},sources:[RULES,AST,CDC_SSI,WHO_CHECK]};
